  "prompt_text": "The figure compares how the optical response of a layered material changes as its thickness decreases from bulk to thin and then to molecularly thin films. In all cases, a green excitation beam creates electrons and holes inside the stacked crystal, and the drawings emphasize how these carriers move and emit light.\n\nIn the bulk sample, carriers appear to spread through the material by diffusion, and emitted photons are reabsorbed and re-emitted inside the slab, labeled as photon recycling. The outgoing light is red-shifted relative to the excitation, shown as a lower frequency output. In the intermediate-thickness film, lateral transport along the layers becomes more directed, and the emitted signal is shown at much higher frequency, marked as roughly three times the excitation, indicating strong nonlinear light generation. In the molecularly thin case, the transport looks confined within a few layers between electrodes, with opposite charges accumulated on the contacts, suggesting an electrically influenced in-plane carrier motion.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that reducing thickness changes the dominant transport and emission mechanisms: thick crystals favor diffusion and photon recycling, thinner films enhance directional propagation and strong harmonic emission, and ultrathin layers behave more like electrically controlled nanoscale channels.",
